index 7a1b2bf..2681f29 100644
--- a/docs/rounding.md
+++ b/docs/rounding.md
@@ -5,10 +5,13 @@ use exact integer arithmetic and then round once.
 
 ## The default mode
 
-The default rounding mode is half-up: an exact half always rounds away from zero.
-So 2.5 rounds to 3, 3.5 rounds to 4 and -2.5 rounds to -3.
+The default rounding mode is half-even (banker's rounding): an exact half rounds to
+the nearest even number, and anything else rounds to the nearest whole number. So
+2.5 rounds to 2, 3.5 rounds to 4 and -2.5 rounds to -2. Finance asked for this so
+that rounding does not lean upwards over many invoices (changelog 0.4.0).
 
-This is the mode used for tax, discounts and proration.
+This is the mode used for tax, discounts, proration, currency translation and the
+other figures that need rounding.
 
 ## Other modes
 
@@ -16,11 +19,23 @@ Callers can pass a mode explicitly:
 
 | Mode | 2.5 | 3.5 | 2.4 | -2.5 |
 |---|---|---|---|---|
+| `half-even` (default) | 2 | 4 | 2 | -2 |
 | `half-up` | 3 | 4 | 2 | -3 |
 | `down` | 2 | 3 | 2 | -2 |
 
+`half-up` always rounds an exact half away from zero. `down` drops the fraction.
+
 ## Sign
 
 Rounding is symmetric. The result for a negative value is always the negative of
 the result for the same positive value, so a credit rounds the same way as the
 charge it reverses.
+
+## Splitting an amount
+
+When an amount has to be split across lines (a discount over the lines it applies
+to, tax over the lines in a rate group, an instalment plan), the parts always add up
+to the amount exactly. Each part is its exact share rounded down or up, and the spare
+units go to the parts with the largest fractions (the earlier line wins a tie). A
+line with no share gets nothing, and splitting a negative amount gives the mirror
+image of splitting the positive one.
